<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Muscogee Confederacy</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1907 CE (approx.) · capital Okmulgee</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A league of self-governing towns in Georgia and Alabama, heirs of the Mississippian chiefdoms, which balanced rival empires for a century before removal west.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The confederacy grew out of the survivors of Mississippian chiefdoms such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/coosa-chiefdom">Coosa</a>, along with refugees from other peoples, who regrouped in towns along the Coosa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tallapoosa-river">Tallapoosa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chattahoochee-river">Chattahoochee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flint-river-georgia">Flint</a> rivers in the 17th century. The English called them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creeks</a> after Ochese Creek, where many lived, and by around 1700 they formed a recognised alliance of Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-creek">Lower Towns</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each town, or talwa, had its own square ground, council and chief, the mico, and towns were classed as white (peace) or red (war). The annual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-corn-ceremony">Green Corn</a> ceremony renewed community life. Leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brims">Brims of Coweta</a> played British, French and Spanish colonies against each other, and the deerskin trade brought wealth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-mcgillivray">Alexander McGillivray</a>, the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a> diplomat from the 1780s, negotiated the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> in 1790.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/creek-war">Creek War</a> of 1813 to 1814 ended with defeat at Horseshoe Bend and huge land cessions. Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a> were forcibly removed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a> in the 1830s, where they set up a government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okmulgee">Okmulgee</a> in 1867. It was suppressed by 1907 but revived later.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/muscogee-confederacy.jpg" alt="Muscogee Confederacy" width="320" height="453"><figcaption>Image: Hodge, Frederick Webb (editor). Handbook of American Indians North of Mexico in Two Parts: Part I. Smithsonian Instit... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Creek_man_p_363_from_Handbook_of_American_Indians_north_of_Mexico_by_Hodge.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1907 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Okmulgee</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Brims, Alexander McGillivray, William McIntosh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Muscogee traditional religion, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Muscogee</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Creek Confederacy, Muscogee Nation, Creek Nation</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=muscogee-confederacy" aria-label="Muscogee Confederacy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2256 891 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Muscogee Confederacy lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2576 1147L2570 1008M2576 1147L2556 1163"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2570 1008h0M2556 1163h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2576 1147h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2576 1147h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Okmulgee. <a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=muscogee-confederacy">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
